WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.797
A plant is building a new hall.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.039
Packing line 3 has to move there, during the summer shutdown of 3 weeks.

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.538
The sponsor has set the goal.

4
00:00:11.282 --> 00:00:13.337
That is what this module is about.

5
00:00:14.118 --> 00:00:17.694
Planning turns a goal into a plan that people believe in.

6
00:00:18.703 --> 00:00:22.824
We follow this one project through the whole module, past four topics.

7
00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:24.853
First the big picture.

8
00:00:25.564 --> 00:00:29.488
The details and the calculations come later, in the lessons.

9
00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:33.078
The first topic is Breakdown and scope.

10
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.185
What has to happen before the line runs again?

11
00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:39.982
Start with the deliverables, not the activities.

12
00:00:40.698 --> 00:00:43.368
What will exist at the end that someone can check?

13
00:00:44.086 --> 00:00:45.317
A floor that is ready.

14
00:00:45.993 --> 00:00:47.456
Power and compressed air.

15
00:00:48.142 --> 00:00:49.593
The line in its place.

16
00:00:50.323 --> 00:00:51.809
And trained operators.

17
00:00:52.934 --> 00:00:55.221
This gives a WBS, a tree.

18
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.293
The project sits at the top, with the deliverables below it.

19
00:01:00.347 --> 00:01:02.414
The work packages hang at the bottom.

20
00:01:03.152 --> 00:01:06.740
One person or a small team can do and finish each one.

21
00:01:07.796 --> 00:01:11.442
And you agree the scope, what the project does and does not do.

22
00:01:12.158 --> 00:01:14.167
Rebuild line 4 while we are at it?

23
00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:16.361
That is not part of it.

24
00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:20.568
The second topic is Schedule and critical path.

25
00:01:21.326 --> 00:01:23.544
Now you put the deliverables in time.

26
00:01:24.527 --> 00:01:27.395
Some work can only start when other work is done.

27
00:01:28.128 --> 00:01:29.498
That is a dependency.

28
00:01:30.161 --> 00:01:34.201
The line can only move once the floor is ready and the power is on.

29
00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:39.873
The new power supply comes from an outside contractor, and it takes longest.

30
00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:42.600
That chain sets the end date.

31
00:01:43.349 --> 00:01:46.692
It is the critical path, and you watch it most closely.

32
00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:50.511
And you set milestones, moments with a decision.

33
00:01:51.332 --> 00:01:54.037
The key one comes at the end of the shutdown.

34
00:01:54.737 --> 00:01:57.082
Does the line go into production, or not?

35
00:01:58.426 --> 00:02:01.073
The third topic is Estimating and buffers.

36
00:02:01.816 --> 00:02:04.417
How long does each piece of work really take?

37
00:02:05.452 --> 00:02:09.446
Ask the mechanics for the best, the worst and the most likely case.

38
00:02:10.198 --> 00:02:14.760
Then you get a range instead of one number, and you see where the uncertainty is.

39
00:02:15.775 --> 00:02:17.935
Turn the hours into weeks as well.

40
00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.910
Effort is the number of hours of work.

41
00:02:21.675 --> 00:02:23.718
Duration is the time on the calendar,

42
00:02:23.811 --> 00:02:26.586
because the mechanics also have their normal job.

43
00:02:27.618 --> 00:02:31.148
And put one visible buffer just before the end of the shutdown,

44
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:34.317
instead of hiding a little extra in every task.

45
00:02:35.662 --> 00:02:38.089
The fourth topic is Risk and resources.

46
00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:40.798
What can go wrong, and who does what?

47
00:02:41.884 --> 00:02:43.904
A risk register lists the risks.

48
00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:48.146
Here, for example, the new switchboard for the power supply.

49
00:02:48.846 --> 00:02:50.146
It may arrive late.

50
00:02:51.208 --> 00:02:53.715
Each risk gets one owner, here the buyer.

51
00:02:54.437 --> 00:02:56.759
And a mitigation, something you do now.

52
00:02:57.478 --> 00:02:59.487
He orders the switchboard this week.

53
00:03:00.503 --> 00:03:01.536
And then the people.

54
00:03:02.255 --> 00:03:05.262
In the shutdown everyone wants the same mechanics.

55
00:03:06.556 --> 00:03:09.226
Put it side by side and you see one plan.

56
00:03:09.998 --> 00:03:11.426
The tree gives the work.

57
00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:13.965
The schedule puts it in time.

58
00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:16.540
The estimates give the duration.

59
00:03:17.258 --> 00:03:19.696
And the risk register says what can go wrong.

60
00:03:20.728 --> 00:03:22.086
The topics hang together.

61
00:03:22.822 --> 00:03:24.610
A mitigation is work too.

62
00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.033
If it is not in the tree, nobody will do it.

63
00:03:28.784 --> 00:03:32.116
So add it as a work package, and put it in the schedule.

64
00:03:33.441 --> 00:03:35.124
This is the map of Projects.

65
00:03:35.868 --> 00:03:38.921
Plan is the second station on the road of a project.

66
00:03:39.943 --> 00:03:42.764
Plan has four topics, each with four levels.

67
00:03:43.748 --> 00:03:47.452
Start at the top, with Breakdown and scope, at the first level.

68
00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.106
At the second level you fix the scope and choose a way of working.

69
00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:56.164
You learn estimating methods and share out the roles.

70
00:03:57.178 --> 00:03:59.605
At the third level you work through the network,

71
00:03:59.825 --> 00:04:02.240
add up uncertainty and plan capacity.

72
00:04:02.928 --> 00:04:06.237
The fourth level tracks the plan and manages the reserves.

73
00:04:07.274 --> 00:04:11.361
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
